WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.840
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.700
You may have noticed, but I am not particularly
Hank shaped.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.580
Hi, I’m Sabrina.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:06.880
I just started university, so, as you may
have guessed, I am falling apart.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:10.679
My love of naps and my hatred of myself has
made it so that the only thing that keeps

00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:14.439
me warm at night is the dumpster fire that
is my life, because as it turns out,

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.420
it's really hard being a human being.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.160
Everybody is stressed out by different things,
depending on their work, their relationships, their age.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:27.760
I mean, babies are just out here trying not
to throw up on everything, and I—also that, too.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.160
As a university student, I’ve got midterms,
presentations, essays, readings, and “suggested

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.020
problems,” but we both know what they mean
by “suggested.”

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.180
All right, I’m not saying that you gots
to do this, I am merely suggesting that you

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.190
do questions one to forty-four or else bad
things might happen.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.080
That’s a weird accent.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.280
And I won’t lie to you, I would probably
be crying myself to sleep if I was getting

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.860
some sleep, but are you ready for the big
plot twist revelation?

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.230
I am the happiest I have ever been in my entire
life.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.321
I know it seems a bit paradoxical, but before
you call stockholm syndrome, consider the

00:00:57.321 --> 00:01:01.221
possibility that we build this false dichotomy
around stress and happiness.

00:01:01.221 --> 00:01:04.100
That happiness exists if and only if there
is no stress.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.250
But I would argue that for most of my life,
the biggest hindrance to my happiness was

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.250
a lack of stress.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.710
Good stress, not social anxiety or a crippling
fear of failure, no.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.510
I am talking about the stress that comes along
with doing something that you love so desperately

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.010
that it fills up every hour of your day, and
sometimes even more.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.250
It’s like when you only have fifteen minutes,
but the TV show you’re trying to watch is

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.970
thirty minutes long—it shouldn’t be possible,
but you always find a way.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.600
Now, I didn’t have that sort of stress growing
up because I could afford to be so lazy.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.300
Privilege!

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.200
But because of that, I never worked very hard.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.479
I could never experience the fulfilment of
pushing yourself to your limit, of success

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:42.450
after spending so long at chipping away at
a challenge.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.830
Granted, that challenge might be a course
where everything is just ¿Que?

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:49.760
And the midterm is tomorrow, but let me put
it this way: I remember sitting in my first

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.520
ever math lecture, taking my first step towards
a math major, and realizing I don’t know

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.460
how to do math.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:59.159
I have made a horrible miscalculation, probably
because I don’t know how to do math.

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:01.970
And that realization should have destroyed
me.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:05.630
My entire life plan is probably gonna fail,
just like me in math class.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:12.220
But instead of collapsing into this little
stress singularity, I remember smiling.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:17.139
Maybe it was just a coping mechanism, but
I like to think that it’s because I am so

00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:21.559
enamored with knowing that there is so much
more to know.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.180
And now here I am, arguably too aware that
I know nothing, but for the first time in

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:29.629
my life, I feel like I am working towards
something.

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:33.379
The factors that are stressing me out, the
readings and the writings, they’re the same

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:35.849
factors that build the framework for my happiness.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:39.379
I can’t spend all of time feeling nostalgic
for hypothetical futures when I have a paper

00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:42.840
due at 11:59 on a reading that I’m not even
sure that I read because I read at least 200

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.560
pages this week, and my eyes can only do this
so much before I accidentally hypnotize myself.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.380
And when I do have free time, I spend what
I have left napping.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:58.180
But what I have left after that, I spend truly
appreciating my life, my friends, and my family.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.980
What I’m trying to say is that I am the
most stressed out I have ever been but the

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:08.760
happiest I could ever hope, and it is so good
to know that those things are not mutually exclusive.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.440
And John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

